WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:06.180
Two weeks ago, I posted a video on why flying
is so expensive, and, people seemed to like

00:00:06.180 --> 00:00:11.080
it, however, they wanted to know, how are
budget airlines so cheap?

00:00:11.080 --> 00:00:14.769
Well it’s another complex answer but an
interesting one.

00:00:14.769 --> 00:00:19.280
For the purposes of simplicity, and to keep
our European friends from feeling left out,

00:00:19.280 --> 00:00:22.130
we’ll focus on the European model of budget
airlines.

00:00:22.130 --> 00:00:27.230
While there are low-cost carriers in North
America, South Africa, India, Asia, and plenty

00:00:27.230 --> 00:00:31.400
of other places, budget airlines really were
first successful in Europe and that’s where

00:00:31.400 --> 00:00:33.020
they’re still most prominent today.

00:00:33.020 --> 00:00:38.180
The magnitude to which airlines like Ryanair
and EasyJet reduce the cost in Europe is also

00:00:38.180 --> 00:00:40.160
much more significant.

00:00:40.160 --> 00:00:44.920
Budget airlines in the US might be 10 or 20%
less expensive than their traditional competitors

00:00:44.920 --> 00:00:50.399
while in Europe a fare on a budget airline
might only cost half or a third of what you

00:00:50.399 --> 00:00:51.920
would pay to a normal airline.

00:00:51.920 --> 00:00:57.590
You can regularly find 2-3 hour flights on
budget airlines for less than 10 euros

00:00:57.590 --> 00:00:59.309
So how do they work?

00:00:59.309 --> 00:01:04.100
Well, unsurprisingly, budget airlines essentially
take every expensive part of the flight and

00:01:04.100 --> 00:01:05.680
make them less expensive.

00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:09.910
The most essential thing an airline needs
is airplanes and it is possible to spend less

00:01:09.910 --> 00:01:10.960
on those.

00:01:10.960 --> 00:01:15.720
In the months and years following 9/11, the
growth of air travel stalled and most airlines

00:01:15.720 --> 00:01:17.910
were simply fighting to survive.

00:01:17.910 --> 00:01:25.170
Meanwhile, Ryanair was thriving and placed
a massive order of 151 737’s from Boeing

00:01:25.170 --> 00:01:27.430
at unbelievably low-prices.

00:01:27.430 --> 00:01:32.570
A circumstance like that luckily isn’t frequent
but budget airlines can place large orders

00:01:32.570 --> 00:01:35.130
at any time which gives them a bulk discount.

00:01:35.130 --> 00:01:39.750
It might seem weird that a budget airline
would buy brand new airplanes, but the newest

00:01:39.750 --> 00:01:43.070
planes are the most efficient which saves
fuel.

00:01:43.070 --> 00:01:48.700
The fuel efficiency of new aircraft offsets
the higher purchase price, so EasyJet (4.0),

00:01:48.700 --> 00:01:53.890
JetBlue (4.7), Ryanair (5.0), and Spirit Airlines
(5.7) all have younger fleets than any major

00:01:53.890 --> 00:01:54.890
airline (KLM 9.4, Air France 11.5, Lufthansa
12.4).

00:01:54.890 --> 00:01:58.810
Budget airlines will also typically have only
one type of plane.

00:01:58.810 --> 00:02:04.960
Ryanair only operates 737’s and EasyJet
only operates the a320 family.

00:02:04.960 --> 00:02:09.710
Having only one type of Aircraft means that
pilots, flight attendants, mechanics, and

00:02:09.710 --> 00:02:14.350
ground staff only need to be trained on one
type of aircraft which saves an enormous amount

00:02:14.350 --> 00:02:16.060
of time and money.

00:02:16.060 --> 00:02:20.750
Within the planes, budget airlines will often
avoid luxuries to keep costs down.

00:02:20.750 --> 00:02:25.320
Ryanair seats, for example, don’t recline
because that increases the initial purchase

00:02:25.320 --> 00:02:28.200
price for seats and also requires more maintenance.

00:02:28.200 --> 00:02:32.990
The seat design also saves time for flight
attendants since there are no seatback pockets

00:02:32.990 --> 00:02:35.010
to clean between flights.

00:02:35.010 --> 00:02:38.850
Flight attendants on budget airlines are often
in the beginning of their careers and receive

00:02:38.850 --> 00:02:40.250
little training.

00:02:40.250 --> 00:02:44.860
Of course they’ll get the required safety
training but only minimal hospitality training.

00:02:44.860 --> 00:02:47.850
They’ll also often serve multiple roles.

00:02:47.850 --> 00:02:51.940
While on the ground, some of the flight attendants
might go to the gate and check tickets while

00:02:51.940 --> 00:02:53.850
others clean the airplane.

00:02:53.850 --> 00:02:57.561
Through this, the airline eliminates three
or four positions that they regularly would

00:02:57.561 --> 00:02:58.561
pay for.

00:02:58.561 --> 00:03:03.440
Onboard, flight attendants are of course responsible
for food and drinks which are almost never

00:03:03.440 --> 00:03:04.440
free.

00:03:04.440 --> 00:03:07.960
Onboard service can be an excellent way for
the airline to make money.

00:03:07.960 --> 00:03:13.140
Many budget airlines also do duty free sales
and Ryanair even sells lottery tickets.

00:03:13.140 --> 00:03:14.560
Let’s talk Airports.

00:03:14.560 --> 00:03:18.690
You’re never going to see Ryanair fly to
London Heathrow or Paris Charles de Gaulle

00:03:18.690 --> 00:03:20.920
because those airports are expensive.

00:03:20.920 --> 00:03:25.130
There’s only a set amounts of flights per
day that can operate out of them so supply

00:03:25.130 --> 00:03:29.590
and demand dictates that landing fees are
high and slots are regulated.

00:03:29.590 --> 00:03:35.650
Instead, Ryanair flies to Gatwick, Stansted,
or Luton in London or Beauvais in Paris, which,

00:03:35.650 --> 00:03:40.270
by the way, is a 80 minute bus ride away from
Paris, isn’t even technically in the same

00:03:40.270 --> 00:03:44.120
region of Paris, and has a website that looks
like this!

00:03:44.120 --> 00:03:49.230
In a lot of cases, Ryanair or other budget
airlines are the only or one of a few airlines

00:03:49.230 --> 00:03:53.380
flying out of an airport which gives them
huge negotiating power.

00:03:53.380 --> 00:03:58.000
Often they will take a small regional airport
a ways away from a city and rebrand it as

00:03:58.000 --> 00:03:59.850
another city airport.

00:03:59.850 --> 00:04:04.540
They can ask for lower landing and takeoff
prices and, if the airport doesn’t comply,

00:04:04.540 --> 00:04:08.370
just leave and the airport will essentially
cease to exist.

00:04:08.370 --> 00:04:12.910
If there isn’t a viable cheap airport available
around a city, budget airlines can also fly

00:04:12.910 --> 00:04:17.560
to regular airports at less busy times when
landing fees and the chance of delay might

00:04:17.560 --> 00:04:18.690
be lower.

00:04:18.690 --> 00:04:20.930
Now let’s get into the nitty-gritty—the
small details.

00:04:20.930 --> 00:04:24.870
The planes of budget airlines tend to operate
all day non-stop.

00:04:24.870 --> 00:04:31.290
For example, in one day, this Ryanair plane
went from Brussels to Copenhagen, Copenhagen

00:04:31.290 --> 00:04:37.110
to Brussels, Brussels to Prague, Prague to
Brussels, Brussels to Nimes, Nimes to Brussels,

00:04:37.110 --> 00:04:41.050
Brussels to Trevioso, then finally Trevioso
to Brussels.

00:04:41.050 --> 00:04:46.420
Budget airlines will schedule only 30-45 minutes
between the landing of one-flight to the take-off

00:04:46.420 --> 00:04:51.750
of another which often leads to delays and
leaves very little time to clean the aircraft.

00:04:51.750 --> 00:04:56.100
This does, however, mean that the airplane
is always making money and passengers are

00:04:56.100 --> 00:04:58.820
not paying for the time it’s sitting around.

00:04:58.820 --> 00:05:01.780
Ever wonder why Southwest doesn’t have reserved
seating?

00:05:01.780 --> 00:05:06.690
Well by having a first come first serve system
for seats, people almost always show up to

00:05:06.690 --> 00:05:10.350
the gate early and line up in an orderly line.

00:05:10.350 --> 00:05:15.910
This way, less time is spent on the ground
boarding and more time in the air flying.

00:05:15.910 --> 00:05:19.380
Another principle of budget airlines is the
point-to-point model.

00:05:19.380 --> 00:05:23.500
Most traditional airlines have hubs where
most or all of their flights go in and out

00:05:23.500 --> 00:05:24.500
of.

00:05:24.500 --> 00:05:29.100
British Airlines has London, Air France has
Paris, KLM has Amsterdam.

00:05:29.100 --> 00:05:33.610
To get to most places on these airlines, you
have to connect through their hubs.

00:05:33.610 --> 00:05:38.140
Budget airlines, on the other hand, try to
have a lot of destinations from everywhere.

00:05:38.140 --> 00:05:43.449
That does mean, however, that many destinations
are served only a few times per week.

00:05:43.449 --> 00:05:47.630
The cheapest budget airlines don’t even
allow for connections between their flights.

00:05:47.630 --> 00:05:51.630
Allowing connections adds cost because then
you have to pay for ground crews to transfer

00:05:51.630 --> 00:05:56.370
bags, create a more complicated ticketing
system, and pay to rebook a passenger if a

00:05:56.370 --> 00:05:59.320
delay in their first flight makes them miss
their second.

00:05:59.320 --> 00:06:03.520
Speaking of ticketing, there’s often no
way to get a ticket from a real person on

00:06:03.520 --> 00:06:04.810
budget airlines.

00:06:04.810 --> 00:06:09.410
Ryanair charges 45 pounds if you fail to print
your boarding pass at home and EasyJet and

00:06:09.410 --> 00:06:13.310
some other carriers have almost all their
check-in’s handled by machines.

00:06:13.310 --> 00:06:16.890
This, once again, cuts down on personnel costs.

00:06:16.890 --> 00:06:22.370
At the airport, these budget airlines won’t
bother using jetways because they’re expensive.

00:06:22.370 --> 00:06:26.660
They’ll use steps and just have passengers
walk across the tarmac or take a bus to the

00:06:26.660 --> 00:06:28.410
plane.

00:06:28.410 --> 00:06:32.160
Budget airlines make a lot of money, if done
right.

00:06:32.160 --> 00:06:36.420
EasyJet (11.15%), Ryanair (24.10%), and Wizz
Air (10.2%) all have higher profit margins

00:06:36.420 --> 00:06:39.860
than Lufthansa (4.03%), British Airways (7.09%),
and Air France (2.15%).

00:06:39.860 --> 00:06:44.260
Many of the traditional airlines have unionized
workers with salary agreements that cannot

00:06:44.260 --> 00:06:48.860
be changed while the budget airlines can hire
anyone and train them in a few months.

00:06:48.860 --> 00:06:52.370
It’s also hard for these traditional airlines
to grow.

00:06:52.370 --> 00:06:56.600
Any route they make has to be one with a lot
of preexisting demand since much of their

00:06:56.600 --> 00:06:59.400
business comes from business travelers.

00:06:59.400 --> 00:07:04.110
Since budget airlines are targeted more towards
tourists, any destination that Ryanair, for

00:07:04.110 --> 00:07:09.009
example, opens up in will become a popular
destination just because it’s possible to

00:07:09.009 --> 00:07:10.860
go there for so cheap.

00:07:10.860 --> 00:07:15.240
Some traditional airlines in Europe are opening
their own budget airlines to get in on this

00:07:15.240 --> 00:07:16.240
profit.

00:07:16.240 --> 00:07:20.790
Air France created Transavia and Lufthansa
created Eurowings, both of which are losing

00:07:20.790 --> 00:07:22.770
massive amounts of money.

00:07:22.770 --> 00:07:26.670
What they seem to forget is that the US went
through this budget airline within an airline

00:07:26.670 --> 00:07:28.600
phase a couple decades ago.

00:07:28.600 --> 00:07:31.310
Delta created Song, which failed.

00:07:31.310 --> 00:07:34.380
US Airways created MetroJet, which failed.

00:07:34.380 --> 00:07:38.780
United created Shuttle, which failed, then
they went into bankruptcy and decided they

00:07:38.780 --> 00:07:41.760
should try again and make Ted, which failed.

00:07:41.760 --> 00:07:42.760
None of these worked!

00:07:42.760 --> 00:07:43.760
Traditional airlines can’t get away from
their labor agreements, honest business practices,

00:07:43.760 --> 00:07:44.760
and devotion to their hubs.

00:07:44.760 --> 00:07:47.710
The reason Ryanair and EasyJet are able to
succeed where others have failed is largely

00:07:47.710 --> 00:07:50.180
because they are so large and flexible.

00:07:50.180 --> 00:07:55.190
They have hundreds of planes, hundreds of
destinations, thousands of employees, and

00:07:55.190 --> 00:07:58.500
negotiating power that allows them to overpower
competitors.

00:07:58.500 --> 00:08:04.150
In the end, for us consumers, any competition
is good competition.

00:08:04.150 --> 00:08:07.940
Even failing budget airlines will bring down
the cost of traditional airlines and allow

00:08:07.940 --> 00:08:10.150
us to travel the world for less.

00:08:10.150 --> 00:08:11.479
Thank you for watching.

00:08:11.479 --> 00:08:13.639
I hope you enjoyed this video.

00:08:13.639 --> 00:08:18.009
